#Review

Checking renders without watching them. A video is reviewed through a
handful of its frames: a contact sheet of frames sampled evenly through
it, ending on the last, and the last frame at full size for looking
closely at detail. One command makes them for every rendered video, so
checking a change is: render, then look at the sheets.

Sheets are written beside the renders, in the media directory, which is
generated output and not checked in.

    VIDEOS = Path("../media/videos/1080p60")
    SHEETS = Path("../media/frames")
    SAMPLES = 4
    THUMB = 540

##Sampling

Frames are taken at the end of each equal slice of the video, so the
first sample is a quarter of the way in -- past any opening fade, when
there is nothing yet to see -- and the last sample is the final frame,
where a beat comes to rest.

    def sample_indices(count: int, samples: int) -> list:
        return sorted({max(0, round((k + 1) * count / samples) - 1) for k in range(samples)})

~example
    sample_indices(240, 4) == [59, 119, 179, 239]
    sample_indices(2, 4) == [0, 1]

##Sheets

A video is read twice: once to learn its length, when the container does
not record it, and once to keep only the sampled frames, so the whole
video is never held in memory at once.

    def frame_count(video: Path) -> int:
        with av.open(str(video)) as container:
            recorded = container.streams.video[0].frames
            return recorded or sum(1 for _ in container.decode(video=0))

The sheet lays the samples side by side at thumbnail size; the last
frame is also kept at full size.

    def contact_sheet(video: Path, sheets: Path) -> list:
        wanted = set(sample_indices(frame_count(video), SAMPLES))
        picks = []
        with av.open(str(video)) as container:
            for index, frame in enumerate(container.decode(video=0)):
                if index in wanted:
                    picks.append(frame.to_image())
        sheet = Image.new("RGB", (THUMB * len(picks), THUMB))
        for k, image in enumerate(picks):
            sheet.paste(image.resize((THUMB, THUMB)), (k * THUMB, 0))
        sheets.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        outputs = [sheets / f"{video.stem}.png", sheets / f"{video.stem}-last.png"]
        sheet.save(outputs[0])
        picks[-1].save(outputs[1])
        return outputs

##Facts

Some things about a render are better read than looked at: how long it
runs, and whether it carries sound. A video's length is its frame count
over its frame rate; its sound is described by the length of its audio
stream, or nothing if it has none.

    def describe(video: Path) -> str:
        with av.open(str(video)) as container:
            stream = container.streams.video[0]
            seconds = frame_count(video) / float(stream.average_rate)
            audio = container.streams.audio
            sound = (f"sound {float(audio[0].duration * audio[0].time_base):.2f}s"
                     if audio else "no sound")
        return f"{video.stem}: {seconds:.2f}s, {sound}"

Run, it makes sheets for every finished render -- the combined movies,
not manim's partial pieces -- and says what each one is.

~run
    for video in sorted(VIDEOS.glob("*.mp4")):
        print(describe(video))
        for output in contact_sheet(video, SHEETS):
            print("  ", output.resolve())

---

#References
    from pathlib import Path

    import av
    from PIL import Image
